WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.160
The RMF process is often described through seven steps: Prepare, Categorize, Select,

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:07.600
Implement, Assess, Authorize, and Monitor.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:11.440
These steps help organizations manage risk across the system lifecycle.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:14.400
Prepare establishes the organizational and system-level context.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:18.100
Teams identify stakeholders, missions, assumptions, common controls,

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:22.085
risk management strategy, and the information needed to make later steps more effective.

00:00:22.085 --> 00:00:25.900
Categorize defines the potential impact if confidentiality, integrity,

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:27.400
or availability are affected.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:31.540
Categorization shapes the level of rigor and helps determine which baseline controls may

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:32.300
be appropriate.

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:35.045
Select identifies the controls needed for the system.

00:00:35.045 --> 00:00:39.570
Implement turns those selected controls into real safeguards, configurations, procedures,

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:42.185
architecture, automation, monitoring, and documentation.

00:00:42.185 --> 00:00:46.485
Assess evaluates whether controls are implemented correctly, operating as intended,

00:00:46.485 --> 00:00:47.985
and producing the desired outcome.

00:00:47.985 --> 00:00:51.900
Authorize is the formal risk decision about whether the system may operate with the

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:52.600
remaining risk.

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:55.685
Monitor keeps the risk picture current after authorization.

00:00:55.685 --> 00:00:59.400
Systems change, vulnerabilities appear, users change, dependencies shift,

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:00.560
and threats evolve.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.935
Continuous monitoring helps the organization keep decisions grounded in current

00:01:03.935 --> 00:01:04.890
information.

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:08.295
Although the steps are often shown in sequence, RMF is not purely linear.

00:01:08.295 --> 00:01:12.000
Findings may cause control changes, monitoring may trigger reassessment,

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:15.700
and new mission needs may require updates to categorization, controls,

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:17.055
or authorization conditions.

00:01:17.055 --> 00:01:19.475
That feedback loop is one of RMF's strengths.

00:01:19.475 --> 00:01:23.500
It lets organizations adjust the risk picture when new information appears instead of

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:26.580
relying on assumptions from an earlier point in the system lifecycle.
